# Line endings.
#
# Default everything to LF in the repository and in the working tree. This
# project is built and run on Linux, and several files break outright with
# CRLF: a shell script copied into a container gets `#!/bin/bash\r`, which
# the kernel reports as "bad interpreter" with an invisible cause. Cloning on
# a Windows box with core.autocrlf=true would otherwise produce exactly that.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*/Dockerfile    text eol=lf

# The canary file's bytes are part of a runtime contract: deployments compare
# what they fetch from raw.githubusercontent.com against a known marker (and
# optionally a sha256). Any end-of-line conversion would change those bytes
# and make every deployment believe its primary uplink had been hijacked.
# Excluded from all conversion, not merely pinned to LF.
canary/canary.txt -text

# The one deliberate exception. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a BOM-less file
# as ANSI, so the em-dashes in this script decoded to CP1252 curly quotes and
# terminated string literals — the launcher would not parse at all. It is
# stored with CRLF and a UTF-8 BOM.
scripts/vlb.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch.
*.png binary
